    "summary": "Acoustic Truckin' shines, epic Other One & Dancin' jam",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a rare acoustic Truckin' with Pigpen’s organ front‑and‑center and a second set that erupts into monster jams on The Other One and Dancin'. Standout moments include Saint Stephen > Not Fade Away > Good Lovin' and the soaring acoustic flow.",
    "review": "September 18, 1970 at the Fillmore East captured one of the Dead’s most eclectic nights. An unexpected acoustic rendition of “Truckin’” – Pigpen’s organ unusually forward in the mix – opens the evening with a warm, intimate vibe that sets up a fiery electric second set. Jerry Garcia is on fire, delivering blistering leads on Saint Stephen and driving the 19‑minute Dancin' jam, while Bob Weir steps into lead‑guitar mode for the “St. Stephen > Not Fade Away > Good Lovin'” sequence. Phil’s bass thunders through a crisp matrix soundboard capture, especially evident in the soaring “The Other One”. The night crescendos with a monster Dancin’ and a generous two‑part Good Lovin’, showcasing tight interplay between Jerry and Bob. Though some audience‑only sections suffer from muffling and dropouts (notably early Candyman and Cryptical), the later soundboard portions are near‑perfect, making this show a must‑listen for anyone chasing that 1970 raw energy.",
